window.docContents[19].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-translations-and-coordinate-permutations":{"contents":"Translation by a fixed vector t is an automorphism of the Hamming cube: it\npreserves the Hamming distance since (t + x) - (t + y) = x - y. Under the\nisomorphism hypercubeIsoHamming, this corresponds to the automorphism\nhypercube.translate of Q[n]. There we had to define xor on HypercubeVert n\nand prove its basic properties (such as HypercubeVert.xor_xor) by hand. On\nFin n → Bool, addition is pointwise xor, and the group structure (associativity,\n0, negation) comes for free from Mathlib, as do the characteristic-two facts once\nwe have the BooleanRing instance above.\n\nTranslation by t (pointwise xor) as an automorphism of the Hamming cube./-- Translation by t (pointwise xor) as an automorphism of the Hamming cube. -/\ndef hypercubeHamming.translate {n : ℕ} (t : Fin n → Bool) :\n    hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n where\n  toEquiv := Equiv.addLeft t\n  map_rel_iff' := by\n    intro x y\n    grind [Equiv.coe_addLeft, hypercubeHamming_adj, hammingDist_eq_hammingNorm]\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercubeHamming.translate_apply {n : ℕ} (t x : Fin n → Bool) :\n    hypercubeHamming.translate t x = t + x := rfl\n\n\nPermuting the coordinates by σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n) also preserves the Hamming\ndistance, and hence is an automorphism of the Hamming cube.\n\nlemma hammingDist_comp_perm {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] {β : Type*}\n    [DecidableEq β] (σ : Equiv.Perm ι) (x y : ι → β) :\n    hammingDist (x ∘ σ) (y ∘ σ) = hammingDist x y := Finset.card_equiv σ (by simp)\n\n\nPermutation of coordinates by σ as an automorphism of the Hamming cube;\nx is sent to x ∘ σ.symm so that the i-th coordinate of the image is the\nσ⁻¹ i-th coordinate of x./-- Permutation of coordinates by σ as an automorphism of the Hamming cube;\nx is sent to x ∘ σ.symm so that the i-th coordinate of the image is the\nσ⁻¹ i-th coordinate of x. -/\ndef hypercubeHamming.permute {n : ℕ} (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) :\n    hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n where\n  toEquiv := σ.arrowCongr (Equiv.refl Bool)\n  map_rel_iff' {x y} := by\n    rw [hypercubeHamming_adj, hypercubeHamming_adj,\n      ← hammingDist_comp_perm σ.symm x y]\n    rfl\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercubeHamming.permute_apply {n : ℕ} (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n))\n    (x : Fin n → Bool) : hypercubeHamming.permute σ x = x ∘ σ.symm := rfl\n\n\nIn the automorphism group the inverse f⁻¹ is by definition f.symm, so the\ninverses of translations and coordinate permutations can be computed by rfl. Since\n-t = t, the inverse of translation by t is translation by t itself, so\ntranslations are involutions (characteristic two again).\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercubeHamming.translate_inv_apply {n : ℕ} (t x : Fin n → Bool) :\n    (hypercubeHamming.translate t)⁻¹ x = t + x := rfl\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercubeHamming.permute_inv_apply {n : ℕ} (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n))\n    (x : Fin n → Bool) : (hypercubeHamming.permute σ)⁻¹ x = x ∘ σ := rfl\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009The Automorphism Group of the Hypercube*","header":"12.6.1. Translations and Coordinate Permutations","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-translations-and-coordinate-permutations"},"/Subgraphs/#chapter-subgraphs":{"contents":"namespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nInformally, a subgraph of a graph G is a graph H with V(H) ⊆ V(G) and\nE(H) ⊆ E(G). We have already seen the subgraph order H ≤ G on SimpleGraph V,\nwhich describes subgraphs with the same vertex set (or more precisely the same\nvertex type). For a general subgraph we need to allow the vertex set to vary. Mathlib\nformalizes this with the type SimpleGraph.Subgraph.\n\nIn this chapter we introduce SimpleGraph.Subgraph and the bounded lattice structure\nof subgraphs. We then look at ways of constructing subgraphs: from vertices, edges\nand walks, by taking induced subgraphs, and by deleting vertices and edges. Since a\nSubgraph is not a SimpleGraph we next consider the ways to convert between the\ntwo (Subgraph.coe, Subgraph.spanningCoe and toSubgraph), spanning subgraphs,\nand the parts of the SimpleGraph API that have to be duplicated for Subgraph.\nSince we now have four different ways of talking about subgraphs, we compare them and\ndiscuss when to use each. As a first application we use the De Bruijn–Erdős theorem\nto extend our greedy colouring bound to infinite graphs. We then introduce maps and\ncomaps of subgraphs, with subgraphs of the Mycielskian as a worked example.\n\nMatchings are subgraphs, so we next define the matching number ν G of a graph,\ncompare it with the vertex cover number τ G (weak duality ν G ≤ τ G), and\ncompute it for the hypercube and the Mycielskian.\n\nThe final section is more advanced: we introduce partial colourings, colourings\nthat need only be valid on a given set of vertices. These are the main tool in our\nproof of Brooks' theorem in Brooks' Theorem.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"9. Subgraphs","id":"/Subgraphs/#chapter-subgraphs"},"/Subgraphs/#section-maps-and-comaps":{"contents":"Subgraph.map is a pushforward operation: given a graph homomorphism f : G →g K,\nbetween G : SimpleGraph V and K : SimpleGraph W, and a subgraph H : G.Subgraph,\nH.map f is the subgraph of K whose vertex set is f '' H.verts and whose edges\nare the images f u — f v of edges u — v of H. Note the contrast with\nSimpleGraph.map (Maps), which takes a plain function: here f\nmust be a homomorphism, since the image has to be a subgraph of the given graph K.\n\n#source Subgraph.map\n\n\nThere is also Subgraph.comap which is a pullback operation: given a homomorphism\nf : G →g K and a subgraph H of K, H.comap f is the subgraph of G whose\nvertex set is the preimage f⁻¹' H.verts and whose edges are those edges u — v of\nG for which f u and f v are adjacent in H.\n\n#source Subgraph.comap\n\n\nAs for simple graphs (Maps), map f and comap f form a Galois\nconnection. Here this holds for every homomorphism f, not just for embeddings: a\nhomomorphism never sends two adjacent vertices to the same vertex, so no edge is\nlost when we map forwards.\n\n#check Subgraph.map_le_iff_le_comap -- H.map f ≤ H' ↔ H ≤ H'.comap f\n\n\nWe have Subgraph.inclusion which gives the graph homomorphism between two subgraphs\nH ≤ K (coerced to SimpleGraph on their respective vertex sets).\n\n#check Subgraph.inclusion -- {H K : G.Subgraph} (h : H ≤ K) : H.coe →g K.coe\n\n\nWe also have Subgraph.hom which gives the graph homomorphism from a subgraph H to\nthe original graph G.\n\n#check Subgraph.hom -- .. (H : G.Subgraph) : H.coe →g G\n\n\nCombining Subgraph.map with Subgraph.hom gives Subgraph.coeSubgraph which\nallows us to take a subgraph of a subgraph coerced to SimpleGraph and get a\nsubgraph of the original graph.\n\n#source Subgraph.coeSubgraph --  G'.coe.Subgraph → G.Subgraph\n\n\nexample {A : G.Subgraph} (B : A.coe.Subgraph) (v w : V) : (A.coeSubgraph B).Adj v w\n    ↔ ∃ (hv : v ∈ A.verts) (hw : w ∈ A.verts), B.Adj ⟨_, hv⟩ ⟨_, hw⟩ :=\n  Subgraph.coeSubgraph_adj B v w\n\n\nCombining Subgraph.comap with Subgraph.hom gives Subgraph.restrict: given\nsubgraphs A and B of G, A.restrict B is the part of B that lies in A, as\na subgraph of A.coe (i.e. with vertex type A.verts).\n\n#source Subgraph.restrict\n\n\nVertices of A are adjacent in A.restrict B iff they are adjacent in both A and\nB.\n\nexample {A B : G.Subgraph} (v w : A.verts) :\n    (A.restrict B).Adj v w ↔ A.Adj v w ∧ B.Adj v w := Iff.rfl\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs","header":"9.4. Maps and Comaps","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-maps-and-comaps"},"/Walks/#section-hamiltonian-graphs-ore-dirac-and-bondy-chv-tal":{"contents":"In this section we prove three classical sufficient conditions for a graph to be\nHamiltonian. The oldest two are Ore's theorem Ø. Ore, Note on Hamilton circuits, Amer. Math. Monthly 67 (1960), no. 1, 55., which says that a\nfinite graph on at least 3 vertices is Hamiltonian if the sum of the degrees of every\npair of non-adjacent vertices is at least the number of vertices in the graph, and\nits immediate (but earlier) corollary Dirac's theorem G. A. Dirac, Some theorems on abstract graphs, Proc. London Math. Soc. (3) 2 (1952), no. 1, 69–81. (minimum\ndegree at least half the number of vertices suffices).\n\nRather than prove Ore's theorem directly we prove the Bondy–Chvátal theorem\nJ. A. Bondy and V. Chvátal, A method in graph theory, Discrete Math. 15 (1976), no. 2, 111–135.: a graph is Hamiltonian iff its closure is, where the\nclosure is obtained by repeatedly joining non-adjacent vertices whose degree sum is\nat least the number of vertices. Ore's theorem falls out immediately, since the\nclosure of an Ore graph is complete and complete graphs are Hamiltonian\n(isHamiltonian_top in Mathlib).\n\nRecall that if u ≠ v then G ⊔ edge u v is the graph obtained from G by adding\nthe edge u — v.\n\nThe proof has four steps.\n\n1. A maximal path (one that cannot be extended at either end) whose endpoints\nsatisfy Ore's degree condition can be closed up into a cycle visiting exactly the\nsame vertices. The tools are Pósa rotations and a counting argument.2. If u and v are distinct non-adjacent vertices whose degree sum is at least\n|V|, then G ⊔ edge u v is Hamiltonian iff G is. Adding an edge can only\nhelp, so the hard part is the converse: a Hamiltonian cycle of G ⊔ edge u v\ngives one of G. If the cycle avoids the new edge u — v, it already lies in\nG. Otherwise, deleting u — v leaves a Hamiltonian path from u to v in G.\nA Hamiltonian path is certainly maximal, so step 1 closes it up into a Hamiltonian\ncycle of G.3. Adding such edges repeatedly, in rounds, shows that a graph is Hamiltonian iff\nits closure is.4. Ore's and Dirac's theorems follow, since the closure of an Ore graph is complete.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks","header":"8.8. Hamiltonian Graphs: Ore, Dirac and Bondy–Chvátal*","id":"/Walks/#section-hamiltonian-graphs-ore-dirac-and-bondy-chv-tal"}});